In article <8948@ucdavis.ucdavis.edu> zerkle@iris.ucdavis.edu (Dan Zerkle) writes:
>In article <12978@pt.cs.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>>
>>shouldn't NetHack 5.0 be lean and mean?  you know, fully featured
>
>On a more serious note, it wouldn't be all THAT hard to reduce the
>size of the executable.  A large part of the executable is text, in
>the form of strings.  This is shown in how neatly the executable file
>will compress in certain archive programs.  You could possibly store
>these in a separate file.  This sort of data might be easier to
>manipulate than a straight executable, since you wouldn't be fooling
>with overlays (maybe).  This sort of data could be swapped in and out
>of expanded/extended RAM in some way, and you could even keep it on
>some sort of disk file, loading in messages as needed.  Of course,
>this would be a lot of work....

Not necessarily!  You could easily write a program to process the source
code for nethack, changing strings to numerical tags and creating a string
tag table in a separate file.  Then, just open the file during run time 
and use a lookup routine to find the write string to write.  Sure it's slower,
but it shouldn't be _too_ bad.  Properly done, you could still develop 
the code using strings and then run it through the symbolizer, which could
read in the existing tags and prevent redundancy. 

